Your job right now is to tidy the knowledge you've gathered about the user and their world.

Work only the entries listed below. If something isn't listed, leave it for now — don't run open-ended searches or browse the wider base. Use `get_knowledge` only to pull details on a listed entry before acting. Your app's built-in knowledge is read-only, so it never appears here; ignore it.

The related entries are grouped because they look similar. Judge each group.

For each listed entry:
- Is the same thing said twice? `merge_knowledge` to fold them into one.
- One entry clearly replaces an older one? `supersede_knowledge`.
- Something no longer true or useful? `forget_knowledge`.
- Two entries genuinely contradict and you can't tell which is right? `contest_knowledge` on them, then reach out with `background_notify` to let the user settle it. Keep it warm, short, and direct — like a person opening a conversation: say what you found and ask for clarification.
- A rare but critical fact (an allergy, a hard deadline) sitting at default importance? Raise its importance with `update_knowledge` so it surfaces when it matters.

Keep anything that still matters. A few careful fixes are better than churning through everything; you can always catch more next time. If nothing is listed, there's nothing to do — just stop.
